{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=6{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =4$,  hence the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}-2(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-8\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (8+16x-2-8x-8x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b6bf7dacc5bab5bf71d2d64863000701f43d494c6c782affda93326f7d4a682"} {"question" "q-8099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}+{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=21{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $21{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+7(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})+12(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9+21+12)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ 42\\alpha  & =21 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b6bf7dacc5bab5bf71d2d64863000701f43d494c6c782affda93326f7d4a682"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=20{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2,$ which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-8+8){e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2P+2Q+10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4$ and $Q=0.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0)+5{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b6bf7dacc5bab5bf71d2d64863000701f43d494c6c782affda93326f7d4a682"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-8109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},$ we have that $\\lambda =1$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}-2(\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x})+(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x}) & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+2-2{x}^{2}-4x+{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b6bf7dacc5bab5bf71d2d64863000701f43d494c6c782affda93326f7d4a682"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=4x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-64236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-93677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-93677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4x+4){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4x+4){e}^{2x}-3(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4x+4-3-6x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-93677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b6bf7dacc5bab5bf71d2d64863000701f43d494c6c782affda93326f7d4a682"} {"question" "q-93677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073216/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed9a61f2a1d193cb62d648e5b5c5f6e2b49b696f6af3408e0c2055fef01fa7d"} {"question" "q-93677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-3{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=4x{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-wor